            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-5958",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-5958"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-5958?s=debian&n=sed&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3C4.9-1%2Bdeb12u1",
              "help": {
                "text": "When sed is invoked with both -i (in-place edit) and --follow-symlinks, the function open_next_file() performs two separate, non-atomic filesystem operations on the same path:  1. resolves symlink to its target and stores the resolved path for determining when output is written, 2. opens the original symlink path (not the resolved one) to read the file.  Between these two calls there is a race window. If an attacker atomically replaces the symlink with a different target during that window, sed will: read content from the new (attacker-chosen) symlink target and write the processed result to the path recorded in step 1. This can lead to arbitrary file overwrite with attacker-controlled content in the context of the sed process.   This issue was fixed in version 4.10.\n\n---\n- sed 4.9-3 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1134495)\n[trixie] - sed 4.9-2+deb13u1\n[bookworm] - sed 4.9-1+deb12u1\n[bullseye] - sed <postponed> (Minor issue; can be fixed in next update)\nhttps://gitweb.git.savannah.gnu.org/gitweb/?p=sed.git;a=commit;h=6b9b43c55ccd3beadbc0094b983c82bdb389f33b\nhttps://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2026/05/13/1\n",
                "markdown": "> When sed is invoked with both -i (in-place edit) and --follow-symlinks, the function open_next_file() performs two separate, non-atomic filesystem operations on the same path:  1. resolves symlink to its target and stores the resolved path for determining when output is written, 2. opens the original symlink path (not the resolved one) to read the file.  Between these two calls there is a race window. If an attacker atomically replaces the symlink with a different target during that window, sed will: read content from the new (attacker-chosen) symlink target and write the processed result to the path recorded in step 1. This can lead to arbitrary file overwrite with attacker-controlled content in the context of the sed process.   This issue was fixed in version 4.10.\n\n---\n- sed 4.9-3 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1134495)\n[trixie] - sed 4.9-2+deb13u1\n[bookworm] - sed 4.9-1+deb12u1\n[bullseye] - sed <postponed> (Minor issue; can be fixed in next update)\nhttps://gitweb.git.savannah.gnu.org/gitweb/?p=sed.git;a=commit;h=6b9b43c55ccd3beadbc0094b983c82bdb389f33b\nhttps://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2026/05/13/1\n\n|                |                                                                          |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/sed@4.9-1?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <4.9-1+deb12u1                                                           |\n| Fixed version  | 4.9-1+deb12u1                                                            |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<4.9-1+deb12u1",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "4.9-1+deb12u1",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/sed@4.9-1?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "2.1",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-6368",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-6368"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-6368?s=debian&n=glibc&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3E0",
              "help": {
                "text": "Calling wordexp with WRDE_APPEND in the GNU C Library version 2.0 to version 2.43 can cause the interface to return invalid memory in the we_wordv member, which on subsequent calls to wordfree may abort the process.\n\n---\n- glibc 2.43-4 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1144252)\n[trixie] - glibc <no-dsa> (Minor issue)\nhttps://sourceware.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=34090\nhttps://sourceware.org/git/?p=glibc.git;a=blob;f=advisories/GLIBC-SA-2026-0014\n",
                "markdown": "> Calling wordexp with WRDE_APPEND in the GNU C Library version 2.0 to version 2.43 can cause the interface to return invalid memory in the we_wordv member, which on subsequent calls to wordfree may abort the process.\n\n---\n- glibc 2.43-4 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1144252)\n[trixie] - glibc <no-dsa> (Minor issue)\nhttps://sourceware.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=34090\nhttps://sourceware.org/git/?p=glibc.git;a=blob;f=advisories/GLIBC-SA-2026-0014\n\n|                |                                                                                       |\n|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/glibc@2.36-9%2Bdeb12u9?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | >0                                                                                    |\n| Fixed version  | not fixed                                                                             |\n"
