            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-34380",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-34380"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-34380?s=debian&n=openexr&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3E0",
              "help": {
                "text": "OpenEXR provides the specification and reference implementation of the EXR file format, an image storage format for the motion picture industry. From 3.2.0 to before 3.2.7, 3.3.9, and 3.4.9, a signed integer overflow exists in undo_pxr24_impl() in src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_pxr24.c at line 377. The expression (uint64_t)(w * 3) computes w * 3 as a signed 32-bit integer before casting to uint64_t. When w is large, this multiplication constitutes undefined behavior under the C standard. On tested builds (clang/gcc without sanitizers), two's-complement wraparound commonly occurs, and for specific values of w the wrapped result is a small positive integer, which may allow the subsequent bounds check to pass incorrectly. If the check is bypassed, the decoding loop proceeds to write pixel data through dout, potentially extending far beyond the allocated output buffer. This vulnerability is fixed in 3.2.7, 3.3.9, and 3.4.9.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1133188)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-q3v8-hw4m-59w5\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/pull/2323\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/f5beec2bd8636102e74460a0b624d3e26efc546f (main)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/b2cebfa1c68e76cb2048ac1c1fbf1b50d196ff9d (v3.4.9-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/a5e5a2eba975b57f77e1c6b6d22ecc49553624e2 (v3.2.7-rc)\n",
                "markdown": "> OpenEXR provides the specification and reference implementation of the EXR file format, an image storage format for the motion picture industry. From 3.2.0 to before 3.2.7, 3.3.9, and 3.4.9, a signed integer overflow exists in undo_pxr24_impl() in src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_pxr24.c at line 377. The expression (uint64_t)(w * 3) computes w * 3 as a signed 32-bit integer before casting to uint64_t. When w is large, this multiplication constitutes undefined behavior under the C standard. On tested builds (clang/gcc without sanitizers), two's-complement wraparound commonly occurs, and for specific values of w the wrapped result is a small positive integer, which may allow the subsequent bounds check to pass incorrectly. If the check is bypassed, the decoding loop proceeds to write pixel data through dout, potentially extending far beyond the allocated output buffer. This vulnerability is fixed in 3.2.7, 3.3.9, and 3.4.9.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1133188)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-q3v8-hw4m-59w5\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/pull/2323\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/f5beec2bd8636102e74460a0b624d3e26efc546f (main)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/b2cebfa1c68e76cb2048ac1c1fbf1b50d196ff9d (v3.4.9-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/a5e5a2eba975b57f77e1c6b6d22ecc49553624e2 (v3.2.7-rc)\n\n|                |                                                                                |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | >0                                                                             |\n| Fixed version  | not fixed                                                                      |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": ">0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "MEDIUM",
                "fixed_version": "not fixed",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-40355",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-40355"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-40355?s=debian&n=krb5&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3C1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u5",
              "help": {
                "text": "In MIT Kerberos 5 (aka krb5) before 1.22.3, there is a NULL pointer dereference if an application calls gss_accept_sec_context() on a system with a NegoEx mechanism registered in /etc/gss/mech. An unauthenticated remote attacker can trigger this, causing the process to terminate in parse_nego_message.\n\n---\n- krb5 1.22.1-2.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1135317)\nhttps://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/2e75f0d9362fb979f5fc92829431a590a130929f\n",
                "markdown": "> In MIT Kerberos 5 (aka krb5) before 1.22.3, there is a NULL pointer dereference if an application calls gss_accept_sec_context() on a system with a NegoEx mechanism registered in /etc/gss/mech. An unauthenticated remote attacker can trigger this, causing the process to terminate in parse_nego_message.\n\n---\n- krb5 1.22.1-2.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1135317)\nhttps://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/2e75f0d9362fb979f5fc92829431a590a130929f\n\n|                |                                                                                        |\n|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/krb5@1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u2?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <1.20.1-2+deb12u5                                                                      |\n| Fixed version  | 1.20.1-2+deb12u5                                                                       |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.20.1-2+deb12u5",
                "cvssV3_severity": "MEDIUM",
                "fixed_version": "1.20.1-2+deb12u5",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/krb5@1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u2?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-40356",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-40356"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-40356?s=debian&n=krb5&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3C1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u5",
              "help": {
                "text": "In MIT Kerberos 5 (aka krb5) before 1.22.3, there is an integer underflow and resultant out-of-bounds read if an application calls gss_accept_sec_context() on a system with a NegoEx mechanism registered in /etc/gss/mech. An unauthenticated remote attacker can trigger this, possibly causing the process to terminate in parse_message.\n\n---\n- krb5 1.22.1-2.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1135317)\nhttps://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/2e75f0d9362fb979f5fc92829431a590a130929f\n",
                "markdown": "> In MIT Kerberos 5 (aka krb5) before 1.22.3, there is an integer underflow and resultant out-of-bounds read if an application calls gss_accept_sec_context() on a system with a NegoEx mechanism registered in /etc/gss/mech. An unauthenticated remote attacker can trigger this, possibly causing the process to terminate in parse_message.\n\n---\n- krb5 1.22.1-2.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1135317)\nhttps://github.com/krb5/krb5/commit/2e75f0d9362fb979f5fc92829431a590a130929f\n\n|                |                                                                                        |\n|----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/krb5@1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u2?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <1.20.1-2+deb12u5                                                                      |\n| Fixed version  | 1.20.1-2+deb12u5                                                                       |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.20.1-2+deb12u5",
                "cvssV3_severity": "MEDIUM",
                "fixed_version": "1.20.1-2+deb12u5",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/krb5@1.20.1-2%2Bdeb12u2?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-42766",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-42766"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-42766?s=debian&n=openssl&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3C3.0.20-1%7Edeb12u2",
              "help": {
                "text": "Issue summary: A specially crafted password-encrypted CMS message can trigger a NULL pointer dereference during CMS decryption.  Impact summary: This NULL pointer dereference leads to an application crash and a Denial of Service.  The CMS PasswordRecipientInfo.keyDerivationAlgorithm field is defined as OPTIONAL in the ASN.1 specification and may therefore be absent in specially crafted inputs. During the password-based CMS decryption the OpenSSL CMS implementation dereferences this field without first checking whether it was present.  An attacker who supplies such a CMS message to an application performing password-based CMS decryption can trigger an application crash, leading to a Denial of Service.  Applications that process password-encrypted CMS messages may be affected.  The FIPS modules in 4.0, 3.6, 3.5, 3.4, and 3.0 are not affected by this issue, as the affected code is outside the OpenSSL FIPS module boundary.\n\n---\n- openssl 3.6.3-1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1139674)\nhttps://openssl-library.org/news/secadv/20260609.txt\nFixed by: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/3ff64913615d648cfbb6a6f1cf5529ae7ea829d7 (openssl-3.0.21)\nFixed by: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/ba699b606969d20a108dda3cfe5422d4cc94eefb (openssl-3.0.21)\n",
                "markdown": "> Issue summary: A specially crafted password-encrypted CMS message can trigger a NULL pointer dereference during CMS decryption.  Impact summary: This NULL pointer dereference leads to an application crash and a Denial of Service.  The CMS PasswordRecipientInfo.keyDerivationAlgorithm field is defined as OPTIONAL in the ASN.1 specification and may therefore be absent in specially crafted inputs. During the password-based CMS decryption the OpenSSL CMS implementation dereferences this field without first checking whether it was present.  An attacker who supplies such a CMS message to an application performing password-based CMS decryption can trigger an application crash, leading to a Denial of Service.  Applications that process password-encrypted CMS messages may be affected.  The FIPS modules in 4.0, 3.6, 3.5, 3.4, and 3.0 are not affected by this issue, as the affected code is outside the OpenSSL FIPS module boundary.\n\n---\n- openssl 3.6.3-1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1139674)\nhttps://openssl-library.org/news/secadv/20260609.txt\nFixed by: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/3ff64913615d648cfbb6a6f1cf5529ae7ea829d7 (openssl-3.0.21)\nFixed by: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/commit/ba699b606969d20a108dda3cfe5422d4cc94eefb (openssl-3.0.21)\n\n|                |                                                                                         |\n|----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/openssl@3.0.15-1~deb12u1?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <3.0.20-1~deb12u2                                                                       |\n| Fixed version  | 3.0.20-1~deb12u2                                                                        |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<3.0.20-1~deb12u2",
                "cvssV3_severity": "MEDIUM",
                "fixed_version": "3.0.20-1~deb12u2",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/openssl@3.0.15-1~deb12u1?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-48994",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-48994"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-48994?s=debian&n=imagemagick&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3C8%3A6.9.11.60%2Bdfsg-1.6%2Bdeb12u11",
              "help": {
                "text": "ImageMagick is free and open-source software used for editing and manipulating digital images. Prior to versions 6.9.13-48 and 7.1.2-24, a missing check of a return value could lead to a heap buffer over-write in the MAT decoder on 32-bit systems. This issue has been patched in versions 6.9.13-48 and 7.1.2-24.\n\n---\n- imagemagick 8:7.1.2.24+dfsg1-1\nhttps://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/security/advisories/GHSA-4v89-6mgq-6rgc\nFixed by: https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/commit/44df3a54af31b8d33fa5e40b4dc61d051c4a5d9a (7.1.2-24)\nFixed by: https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick6/commit/662a1667d115a65b22a3792755431fc9c1f31d89 (6.9.13-49)\n",
                "markdown": "> ImageMagick is free and open-source software used for editing and manipulating digital images. Prior to versions 6.9.13-48 and 7.1.2-24, a missing check of a return value could lead to a heap buffer over-write in the MAT decoder on 32-bit systems. This issue has been patched in versions 6.9.13-48 and 7.1.2-24.\n\n---\n- imagemagick 8:7.1.2.24+dfsg1-1\nhttps://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/security/advisories/GHSA-4v89-6mgq-6rgc\nFixed by: https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/commit/44df3a54af31b8d33fa5e40b4dc61d051c4a5d9a (7.1.2-24)\nFixed by: https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick6/commit/662a1667d115a65b22a3792755431fc9c1f31d89 (6.9.13-49)\n\n|                |                                                                                                               |\n|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/imagemagick@8%3A6.9.11.60%2Bdfsg-1.6%2Bdeb12u2?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <8:6.9.11.60+dfsg-1.6+deb12u11                                                                                |\n| Fixed version  | 8:6.9.11.60+dfsg-1.6+deb12u11                                                                                 |\n"
