            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-53532",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-53532"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-53532?s=debian&n=openexr&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3E0",
              "help": {
                "text": "OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. In versions 3.4.0 through 3.4.12, a crafted HTJ2K-compressed EXR file causes an unconditional process abort in any application that calls exr_start_read() on untrusted input, resulting in denial of service. The crash is triggered by a QCD marker whose lower five bits are zero, which OpenEXR passes into the vendored OpenJPH library while constructing the codestream and evaluating its quantization delta parameters. OpenJPH uses an assertion rather than a recoverable error to validate those bits, so any invalid value calls abort() directly and cannot be intercepted by surrounding error handling, a problem compounded by OpenEXR wrapping only its internal HT header parser in error handling while leaving the later codestream read and construction calls unprotected. This issue has been resolved in version 3.4.13.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-2f85-52wj-hc3c\n",
                "markdown": "> OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. In versions 3.4.0 through 3.4.12, a crafted HTJ2K-compressed EXR file causes an unconditional process abort in any application that calls exr_start_read() on untrusted input, resulting in denial of service. The crash is triggered by a QCD marker whose lower five bits are zero, which OpenEXR passes into the vendored OpenJPH library while constructing the codestream and evaluating its quantization delta parameters. OpenJPH uses an assertion rather than a recoverable error to validate those bits, so any invalid value calls abort() directly and cannot be intercepted by surrounding error handling, a problem compounded by OpenEXR wrapping only its internal HT header parser in error handling while leaving the later codestream read and construction calls unprotected. This issue has been resolved in version 3.4.13.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-2f85-52wj-hc3c\n\n|                |                                                                                |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | >0                                                                             |\n| Fixed version  | not fixed                                                                      |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": ">0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "not fixed",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-59184",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59184"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-59184?s=debian&n=openexr&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3E0",
              "help": {
                "text": "OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. Versions before 3.2.11, 3.3.0 through 3.3.12, and 3.4.0 through 3.4.13 allow a crafted EXR with a nonzero dataWindow.min to make TypedFlatImageChannel::row() return an invalid heap pointer, causing out-of-bounds or use-after-free writes. This occurs when an application writes rows through FlatHalfChannel::row(). Affected consumers are tools, converters, render pipeline components, or image-processing services that accept untrusted EXR files and use FlatHalfChannel::row() on loaded images. This issue is fixed in versions 3.2.11, 3.3.13, and 3.4.14.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-pqp9-558c-453q\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/55b7958ecb5ac32c427ff39c1e063f6f7bbee77c (v3.4.14-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/aef02224ba282a802de65d16c49c1cdb82089dec (v3.3.13-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/37f03b6ed90f3dd9910f31de3a40f25f2bc2aca1 (v3.2.11-rc)\n",
                "markdown": "> OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. Versions before 3.2.11, 3.3.0 through 3.3.12, and 3.4.0 through 3.4.13 allow a crafted EXR with a nonzero dataWindow.min to make TypedFlatImageChannel::row() return an invalid heap pointer, causing out-of-bounds or use-after-free writes. This occurs when an application writes rows through FlatHalfChannel::row(). Affected consumers are tools, converters, render pipeline components, or image-processing services that accept untrusted EXR files and use FlatHalfChannel::row() on loaded images. This issue is fixed in versions 3.2.11, 3.3.13, and 3.4.14.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-pqp9-558c-453q\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/55b7958ecb5ac32c427ff39c1e063f6f7bbee77c (v3.4.14-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/aef02224ba282a802de65d16c49c1cdb82089dec (v3.3.13-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/37f03b6ed90f3dd9910f31de3a40f25f2bc2aca1 (v3.2.11-rc)\n\n|                |                                                                                |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | >0                                                                             |\n| Fixed version  | not fixed                                                                      |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": ">0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "not fixed",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-59186",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59186"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-59186?s=debian&n=openexr&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=12&vr=%3E0",
              "help": {
                "text": "OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. In versions before 3.2.11, 3.3.0 through 3.3.12, and 3.4.0 through 3.4.13, a crafted tiled EXR can trigger a heap out-of-bounds write on 32-bit/ILP32 builds when read through the public TiledRgbaInputFile RGBA API. The file uses a small 40x40 dataWindow but a 65537x65537 tile size. On ILP32, the Array2D<Rgba> tile-conversion buffer size calculation overflows, allocates a much smaller heap buffer, and tile decode writes past that allocation. This issue is fixed in versions 3.2.11, 3.3.13, and 3.4.14.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\n[trixie] - openexr <no-dsa> (Minor issue)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-f667-c4wm-c8gq\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/71907b44ce9a1b05bf3934b8a7821752750731ab (v3.4.14-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/b1a5887372772d79328f4eb42b7f86352a38170b (v3.3.13-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/904141d3a1f86327ad1e2b93fc92ce2dd5881d34 (v3.2.11-rc)\n",
                "markdown": "> OpenEXR is the reference implementation and specification for the EXR image format, widely used in the motion picture industry. In versions before 3.2.11, 3.3.0 through 3.3.12, and 3.4.0 through 3.4.13, a crafted tiled EXR can trigger a heap out-of-bounds write on 32-bit/ILP32 builds when read through the public TiledRgbaInputFile RGBA API. The file uses a small 40x40 dataWindow but a 65537x65537 tile size. On ILP32, the Array2D<Rgba> tile-conversion buffer size calculation overflows, allocates a much smaller heap buffer, and tile decode writes past that allocation. This issue is fixed in versions 3.2.11, 3.3.13, and 3.4.14.\n\n---\n- openexr 3.4.14-0.1\n[trixie] - openexr <no-dsa> (Minor issue)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/security/advisories/GHSA-f667-c4wm-c8gq\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/71907b44ce9a1b05bf3934b8a7821752750731ab (v3.4.14-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/b1a5887372772d79328f4eb42b7f86352a38170b (v3.3.13-rc)\nhttps://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr/commit/904141d3a1f86327ad1e2b93fc92ce2dd5881d34 (v3.2.11-rc)\n\n|                |                                                                                |\n|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/openexr@3.1.5-5?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | >0                                                                             |\n| Fixed version  | not fixed                                                                      |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": ">0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "not fixed",
