              "help": {
                "text": "Integer wraparound in PostgreSQL fuzzystrmatch allows a user to direct writes to a huge range of addresses, executing arbitrary code as the operating system user running the database, via extreme inputs to SQL function levenshtein() or levenshtein_less_equal().  Versions before PostgreSQL 18.5, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, and 14.24 are affected.\n\n---\n- postgresql-18 18.6-1\n- postgresql-17 <removed>\n- postgresql-15 <removed>\n- postgresql-13 <unfixed>\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/support/security/CVE-2026-15742/\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-186-1711-1615-1519-1424-and-19-beta-3-released-3365/\n",
                "markdown": "> Integer wraparound in PostgreSQL fuzzystrmatch allows a user to direct writes to a huge range of addresses, executing arbitrary code as the operating system user running the database, via extreme inputs to SQL function levenshtein() or levenshtein_less_equal().  Versions before PostgreSQL 18.5, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, and 14.24 are affected.\n\n---\n- postgresql-18 18.6-1\n- postgresql-17 <removed>\n- postgresql-15 <removed>\n- postgresql-13 <unfixed>\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/support/security/CVE-2026-15742/\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-186-1711-1615-1519-1424-and-19-beta-3-released-3365/\n\n|                |                                                                                                |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Package        | pkg:deb/debian/postgresql-15@15.10-0%2Bdeb12u1?os_distro=bookworm&os_name=debian&os_version=12 |\n| Affected range | <15.19-0+deb12u1                                                                               |\n| Fixed version  | 15.19-0+deb12u1                                                                                |\n"
